\title{A Complete Classification of the Repaired Anti-Fibonacci Greedy Process}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study a greedy integer sequence defined by a \emph{global additive avoidance}
rule: starting from $a_0 = 1$, each successive term is chosen to be the least value
that strictly exceeds the current term and that cannot be written as a sum of two
earlier terms. This rule repairs a degenerate literal formulation of an
``anti-Fibonacci'' process whose original statement excluded a single integer and
collapsed to the constant sequence. We prove that the repaired process is completely
rigid: it admits exactly one trajectory, namely the positive odd integers
$a_n = 2n+1$. From this exact law we derive a constant first difference of two, strict
monotonicity, oddness of every term, an exact identification of the value set with the
set of all odd natural numbers, and exact prefix cardinalities. The central mechanism
is that sums of odd numbers are even, so the odd numbers are sum-free under the induced
dynamics; the unique even near-miss above each term is precisely blocked by the sum of
the seed and the current term. The result upgrades a previously known exponential
one-step ceiling to an exact linear law.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Sequences of integers defined by greedy avoidance conditions occupy a rich corner of
additive combinatorics. The prototype is the Fibonacci recurrence, in which each term
is the \emph{sum} of its two predecessors. Reversing that spirit yields
``anti-Fibonacci'' rules in which each term must \emph{avoid} being expressible as a sum
drawn from the sequence's own history. Such constructions are close cousins of sum-free
sets and Sidon sets, and they inherit the fundamental tension between a set and its own
sumset.

A recurring hazard with these definitions is degeneracy. A rule may be stated with
apparent precision yet fail to define the intended object---by excluding the wrong
quantity, or by permitting a trivial escape that collapses the sequence to a constant.
The guiding principle of this work is: \emph{repair the definition before studying
asymptotics.} A literal earlier rule excluded a single integer at each stage and,
lacking a strict-growth requirement, admitted the constant-one sequence
$1, 1, 1, \dots$ as a fixed point.

We adopt a repaired rule with two features. First, each new term must \emph{strictly
exceed} the current term, guaranteeing genuine progress. Second, the forbidden set is
\emph{global additive}: the term must avoid every value expressible as a sum
$a_i + a_j$ of two earlier terms (indices allowed to coincide). Under this repair we
establish complete rigidity.

\begin{theorem}[Rigidity, informal]
A sequence $a \colon \NN \to \NN$ satisfies the repaired rule if and only if
$a_n = 2n+1$ for all $n$. In particular the rule has a unique trajectory, the positive
odd integers $1, 3, 5, 7, \dots$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $a \colon \NN \to \NN$ denotes a candidate sequence and $n \in \NN$ a stage
index.

\begin{definition}[Prior pair sums]
For a sequence $a$ and a bound $n$, the \emph{prior pair-sum set} is
\[
  P_n(a) = \{\, a_i + a_j : 0 \le i < n,\ 0 \le j < n \,\}.
\]
It collects every value obtainable as the sum of two terms with indices strictly below
$n$ (the indices may coincide). Membership unfolds as
\[
  s \in P_n(a) \iff \exists\, i < n,\ \exists\, j < n,\ a_i + a_j = s.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Admissibility]
A value $z$ is \emph{admissible after stage $n$} for $a$ if
\[
  \Adm(a, n, z) \iff (a_n < z) \ \wedge \ (z \notin P_{n+1}(a)).
\]
The bound $n+1$ means the forbidden sums are formed from $a_0, \dots, a_n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Greedy successor]
A value $z$ is the \emph{greedy successor after stage $n$} if
\[
  \Greedy(a, n, z) \iff \Adm(a, n, z) \ \wedge \ (\forall w,\ \Adm(a, n, w) \Rightarrow z \le w).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Repaired rule]
A sequence $a$ \emph{satisfies the repaired rule} if
\[
  \Repaired(a) \iff (a_0 = 1) \ \wedge \ (\forall n,\ \Greedy(a, n, a_{n+1})).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Canonical trajectory]
The \emph{canonical trajectory} is $c_n = 2n+1$, i.e. $1, 3, 5, 7, \dots$.
\end{definition}

The goal is to prove $\Repaired(a) \iff a = c$.

\section{The Classification Theorem}

\subsection{Locality of the rule}

\begin{lemma}[Sumset locality]\label{lem:sumloc}
If $a_i = b_i$ for all $i < n$, then $P_n(a) = P_n(b)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Membership $s \in P_n(a)$ unfolds to witnesses $i, j < n$ with $a_i + a_j = s$.
Substituting $a_i = b_i$ and $a_j = b_j$ (valid since $i, j < n$) gives the same
witnesses for $b$, and conversely.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Admissibility locality]\label{lem:admloc}
If $a_i = b_i$ for all $i \le n$, then $\Adm(a, n, z) \iff \Adm(b, n, z)$ for every $z$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The first conjunct $a_n < z$ becomes $b_n < z$ via $a_n = b_n$. The second conjunct
references $P_{n+1}$, unchanged by Lemma~\ref{lem:sumloc} applied with bound $n+1$
(whose hypothesis is exactly $a_i = b_i$ for $i \le n$).
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Greedy locality]\label{lem:greloc}
If $a_i = b_i$ for all $i \le n$, then $\Greedy(a, n, z) \iff \Greedy(b, n, z)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from Lemma~\ref{lem:admloc}, since the greedy predicate is built from
admissibility of $z$ and of all competitors $w$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Uniqueness of the greedy successor}

\begin{lemma}[Uniqueness]\label{lem:unique}
If $\Greedy(a, n, x)$ and $\Greedy(a, n, y)$, then $x = y$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Since $x$ is least admissible and $y$ is admissible, $x \le y$; symmetrically
$y \le x$. Antisymmetry gives $x = y$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The canonical trajectory obeys the rule}

The essential arithmetic fact is that $c_i + c_j = (2i+1)+(2j+1) = 2(i+j+1)$ is even.

\begin{lemma}[Odd successor is admissible]\label{lem:adm}
For every $n$, $\Adm(c, n, c_{n+1})$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
First, $c_n = 2n+1 < 2n+3 = c_{n+1}$. Second, if $c_{n+1} \in P_{n+1}(c)$ then
$2n+3 = (2i+1)+(2j+1)$ is even, contradicting that $2n+3$ is odd.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Odd successor is least]\label{lem:least}
For every $n$, $\Greedy(c, n, c_{n+1})$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Admissibility holds by Lemma~\ref{lem:adm}. For minimality, let $w$ be admissible, so
$2n+1 < w$; we show $2n+3 \le w$. If instead $2n+1 < w < 2n+3$ then $w = 2n+2 = 1 + (2n+1)
= c_0 + c_n$, so $w \in P_{n+1}(c)$ via witnesses $i=0$, $j=n$, contradicting
admissibility. Hence $w \ge 2n+3 = c_{n+1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:canon}
$\Repaired(c)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$c_0 = 1$, and $\Greedy(c, n, c_{n+1})$ for all $n$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:least}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Rigidity}

\begin{theorem}[Classification]\label{thm:main}
For every sequence $a$,
\[
  \Repaired(a) \iff a = c, \qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad a_n = 2n+1 \text{ for all } n.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) is Proposition~\ref{prop:canon}.

($\Rightarrow$) Assume $\Repaired(a)$ and prove $a_n = c_n$ by strong induction. For
$n = 0$, $a_0 = 1 = c_0$. For $n+1$, the induction hypothesis gives $a_i = c_i$ for
$i \le n$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:greloc}, $\Greedy(a, n, a_{n+1})$ transports to
$\Greedy(c, n, a_{n+1})$. Since $\Greedy(c, n, c_{n+1})$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:least},
uniqueness (Lemma~\ref{lem:unique}) yields $a_{n+1} = c_{n+1}$.
\end{proof}

\section{Structural Corollaries}

All of the following follow from Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.

\begin{corollary}[Exact value]
If $\Repaired(a)$ then $a_n = 2n+1$ for all $n$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Constant increment]
If $\Repaired(a)$ then $a_{n+1} = a_n + 2$; the sequence is an arithmetic progression
with common difference two.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Oddness]
If $\Repaired(a)$ then every $a_n$ is odd.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Strict monotonicity]
If $\Repaired(a)$ then $a$ is strictly increasing.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Each step increases the value by $2 > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Value set]
If $\Repaired(a)$ then $\{\, a_n : n \in \NN \,\} = \{\, x \in \NN : x \text{ odd} \,\}$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Each $a_n = 2n+1$ is odd; conversely any odd $x = 2m+1$ equals $a_m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Exact prefix cardinality]
If $\Repaired(a)$ then the image of $\{0, \dots, n-1\}$ has exactly $n$ elements.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Strict monotonicity makes $a$ injective, so $n$ distinct indices map to $n$ distinct
values.
\end{proof}

The value-set corollary exhibits the odd numbers as sum-free under the induced dynamics:
no odd number is a sum of two odd numbers, so the trajectory never collides with its
forbidden sumset. The constant-increment corollary sharpens a previously known
exponential one-step ceiling to an exact linear law.

\section{Discussion}

The literal predecessor rule was ill-posed: without strict growth it admitted the
constant sequence, and with only a single excluded integer it lacked the additive
strength to constrain the trajectory. The repair introduces two coupled requirements
---strict increase and global additive avoidance---and the classification shows they are
exactly calibrated: dropping either restores degeneracy or multiplicity. Together they
carve out a one-dimensional solution set: a single sequence.

A subtle feature is that the seed $a_0 = 1$ does double duty. It initializes the
sequence and also supplies, at every stage $n$, the sum $1 + a_n$ that blocks the even
integer immediately above the current term. Without this block the greedy rule might
select the even near-miss and the odd structure would fail.

Greedy algorithms are notorious for local, not global, optimality. Here the greedy rule
is globally perfect: it produces the densest sum-free progression (hitting every odd
number) with perfectly non-colliding prefixes. Uniqueness is a strong form of
well-posedness: the repaired rule is not merely well-defined but rigid, its solution set
a single point.

\section{Future Directions}

The repaired repeated-summand rule is completely classified: its unique trajectory is
$a_n = 2n+1$, with strict monotonicity, range equal to all odd naturals, and exact
prefix cardinalities. The main remaining extensions are:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Distinct-summand repair.} Replace repeated-index pair sums by sums from
  distinct earlier indices; determine and classify the resulting trajectory.
  \item \textbf{Higher-order forbidden sums.} Forbid sums of exactly $r$ earlier values;
  establish existence, uniqueness, and growth as a function of $r$.
  \item \textbf{Alternative seeds.} Start from an arbitrary positive seed; classify when
  the process becomes an arithmetic progression and when transient behavior persists.
  \item \textbf{Finite-prefix sumset structure.} Prove exact descriptions and
  cardinalities for the restricted pair-sum sets of the canonical trajectory.
  \item \textbf{Asymptotic density.} Upgrade the range classification to a proof that the
  value set has natural density $1/2$.
  \item \textbf{Stability under weakened greediness.} Study trajectories selecting any
  admissible value within a fixed additive error of the least one, and bound their
  densities.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

A greedy rule avoiding all sums of its own history, once repaired to strict growth and
global additive avoidance, admits a unique trajectory: the positive odd integers
$a_n = 2n+1$. The proof rests on a single observation---odd plus odd is even---amplified
by the seed blocking the lone even near-miss above each term. From the exact law flow
strict monotonicity, oddness, the identification of the value set with all odd numbers,
and exact prefix cardinalities. The example illustrates how repairing an ill-posed
definition can convert an exponential worst-case fear into an exact, rigid linear law.

\end{document}

